**A row with an empty measurement renders as "asserted, not measured" — in red, deliberately.** A demo is not a measurement. A stakeholder's confidence is not a measurement. Elapsed time is not a measurement.

## Why the disagreement pattern matters more than the rate

92% clustered on one exception type is a **fixable gap** — a missing rule, probably already in the exception register, possibly a day's work. 92% scattered randomly is a **capability ceiling** — no amount of iteration will move it. They look identical in a summary metric and demand opposite decisions.

Which is why the next table exists: **clustered or scattered is not a judgement call, it is a count.**

<!-- table:autonomy-ledger.by-exception role=register id=Ex id -->

## Agreement by exception class

One row per exception the shadow run touched. The practice asks for agreement
**by exception class**, not a single headline rate, because the headline rate
cannot tell you which of the two situations above you are in.

| Ex id | Run date | Workflow | Cases seen | Disagreements | Rate | Fixable by a rule? | Rule holder |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

`Ex id` cites the exception register. A disagreement on an exception that is
not in the register is the more interesting finding — it means the shadow run
found a case discovery missed, and it belongs in `exception-register.md` before
it belongs here.

`Fixable by a rule?` is the column the rung decision turns on. If the rule
holder can state the rule, the gap is a day's work. If nobody can, it is a
capability ceiling and the rung does not move.

**Concentration is derived.** The dashboard computes what share of
disagreements sit in the top exception class and calls it clustered or
scattered. Do not type the word — a count is harder to argue with than an
impression, and the impression is usually the more optimistic of the two.

